{
  "case_id": "2019-18-565",
  "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0000-0007",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "my client is now facing well in excess of $140 million in -- in -- in an award"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Phillips, this has been the rule for some time in the Seventh Circuit -- in the Second Circuit --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Phillips, even if we're looking just to this contract, these parties, you said, well, look, there is the Second Circuit, but we have the Fifth Circuit on our side."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What if we think the text can be read your way, but it can also be read the other way? What should we do then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And so, if we're talking in terms of contract, why does that make any sense to view it in any other way than to say the charterer picks, the charterer has the expense, risk, and peril of lighterage --and lighterage, in my mind, can include all the charges related to the transfer to a safe berth."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "exceptions clause, which says they would be responsible for any loss or damage resulting from the perils of the sea."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Back to the text, it does say \"safe place or wharf which shall be designated and procured by the charterer.\" So the words \"designated and procured\" are not just the place but the fact that it's safe as well."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Ross",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The parties here chose the first traditional type of clause, which lacks any due diligence language. By the time they had done so, courts, arbitrators, and scholars on both sides of the Atlantic had for decades construed the safe-berth clause as a warranty that the charterer would choose a port that is actually safe, not merely one that the charterer believes to be safe after the exercise of due diligence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, this is a contract case, but Mr. Phillips has argued for something like a due diligence, right? I think of it as really a negligence-type standard. You've argued for something more like strict liability, right, that it's a warranty, absolute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Sir Bernard Eder amicus brief",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0001-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's exactly the example that the Sir Bernard Eder amicus brief gives."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I mean, do you think we can sort of take judicial notice of the fact that an anchor popping up like this in a port that is very heavily used is more foreseeable than a big storm?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I mean, arbitrators don't have to interpret the law the same way a court does, and to the extent they do it, it's not reviewable by a court. So how much can we read into arbitration decisions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Private Respondents",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I thought I would come at this from the perspective that Justice Alito asked about. What if we regard the text as in equipoise? It says safe, and that could mean different things in different contexts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Who is responsible -- who is responsible for negligence of the master? Is the master an employee of the ship's owner or is --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Private Respondents",
      "case_id": "2019-18-565",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2740cc3996bd637f9522eaad581b4f45e87943a0790653e5a39a94740a0128f0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2740cc3996bd-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It's not -- it's not suddenly. That's what they've bargained for. It's a condition of the port."
    },
    {
